Superficial Folliculitis
Common Infections and Afflictions
Follicle-centered pustules, Erythematous base, Shaving or friction trigger

VISIT CONTEXT
Case ID: CIA-040
Reason for Visit: Sudden eruption of painful, itchy pustules across the lower extremities.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a patient reporting a rapid, widespread eruption of highly pruritic, tender pustules following hair removal. The sudden, explosive nature of the lesions has caused acute distress and valid fear of an aggressive, spreading staphylococcal infection or early cellulitis. An urgent home assessment is clinically mandated to physically evaluate the depth of the infection, definitively exclude deeper abscess formation (furunculosis/carbuncles) that would require surgical incision, and initiate immediate, targeted antimicrobial therapy. Delaying this assessment poses an unacceptable risk of allowing a superficial infection to rapidly progress into deeper dermal structures, strictly fulfilling the criteria for an urgent after-hours MBS assessment (MBS 585-600).

SUBJECTIVE
Chief Complaint: My legs broke out overnight in hundreds of painful, itchy whiteheads after I shaved, and I am terrified it is a serious infection.
History of Present Illness: Symptoms initiated 24 hours ago, strictly correlating with a recent history of shaving the lower legs followed by wearing tight clothing (workout leggings). The patient reports the rapid eruption of multiple small, raised erythematous papules and pustules. A central hair follicle is visible within each pustule. The patient experiences mild localized pruritus and tenderness, specifically exacerbated by the friction of clothing. The lesions exhibit a clustered distribution exclusively in the areas of recent friction/shaving. The patient notes some spontaneous rupture of lesions, leaving a tiny crust. Denies systemic fever, chills, rigors, or rapidly expanding deep red rashes.
Functional Status: Independent, but currently avoiding walking to prevent friction against the lesions.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone. High acute anxiety regarding a potential drug-resistant infection (MRSA).

OBJECTIVE
Vitals: BP: 120/78 mmHg | HR: 84 bpm | Temp: 37.0 C | RR: 16 bpm | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 70 Kg
General: Alert, well-perfused. Sitting with legs exposed, visibly uncomfortable.
Recent/Relevant Labs: None available. A bacterial swab culture confirming Staphylococcus aureus if refractory to conservative care would assist in confirming the diagnosis, but empiric management is indicated acutely.
Physical Exam: Dermatological examination of the bilateral lower extremities reveals dozens of 1-3mm follicle-centered pustules. Each pustule is seated on a discrete erythematous base. The lesions are exclusively located in the areas previously subjected to the shaving/friction trigger. The surrounding epidermis between the individual pustules is completely intact, normal-colored, and uninflamed. There is an absolute lack of deeper induration, fluctuance, or abscess formation. No regional lymphangitis (red streaking) or significant tender lymphadenopathy.
Environmental Observations: Used razors visible in the shower cubicle.
Medication Reconciliation: No regular medications. Patient has applied a heavy, occlusive body butter over the rash, exacerbating the follicular blockage.

ASSESSMENT
Primary Diagnosis: Superficial Folliculitis - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:
Cellulitis - Definitively ruled out as the erythema is strictly isolated to the immediate base of each individual hair follicle without contiguous, spreading dermal inflammation, and there are no systemic signs of toxicity.
Furunculosis (Boils) - Ruled out clinically by the lack of large, deep, firm, fluctuant, and exquisitely tender inflammatory nodules requiring drainage.
Herpes Simplex Virus - Unlikely given the lack of grouped, umbilicated vesicles on a singular erythematous base, the definitive presence of pustules centered exactly on hair follicles, and the explicit mechanical trigger.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY
The explosive onset of widespread pustules post-shaving provoked extreme patient anxiety regarding severe cutaneous infection. Urgent home assessment was essential to perform a careful palpation of the lesions, definitively ruling out deep-seated staphylococcal abscesses or rapidly spreading cellulitis. By confirming a benign, superficial mechanical and bacterial inflammation of the follicles, providing immediate topical antiseptics, and correcting detrimental hygiene practices (occlusive moisturizers), a likely emergency department presentation for suspected MRSA was averted. This clinical triage and reassurance safely managed the acute episode in the home, satisfying the criteria for MBS 585-600.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN
Clinical Interventions: Instructed the patient to immediately wash the area with an antibacterial soap (e.g., Chlorhexidine 4% wash) twice daily. Prescribed topical Mupirocin 2% ointment to be applied specifically to the larger, more tender pustules twice daily for 5 days. Oral antibiotics are withheld unless the infection fails to resolve with topical management.
Positive Behavioral Change: To prevent the physical trapping of bacteria inside the pores, entirely cease applying thick, heavy lotions or oils to your legs. These occlusive products act like a physical cork, sealing the bacteria and sweat inside the damaged hair follicle, which directly creates the perfect incubator for these pustules to rapidly multiply.
Home-Based Services: None required.
DME/Safety: None required.
Follow-up: Review with regular General Practitioner in 5-7 days if the pustules enlarge, become deeply painful, or fail to resolve.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN
Present to the Emergency Department immediately if the redness between the spots connects into a large, rapidly spreading, hot patch, if the spots merge into a large, highly painful lump, or if you develop a sudden fever.

PATIENT INFORMATION
Disclaimer: Diagnosis is clinical; recurrent episodes may require swabbing to identify specific bacterial sensitivities.
Clinical Course: The acute tenderness will resolve rapidly within 48 hours of initiating the antibacterial wash. The pustules will crust and heal completely over 7 to 10 days without scarring.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE
Seek medical attention if the spots persist beyond two weeks, or if you notice similar spots developing on family members.

PATIENT EDUCATION
Treatment Explanation: Shaving created microscopic cuts at the opening of each hair follicle. Normal skin bacteria entered these cuts, and the tight clothing rubbed them deep into the pore, causing hundreds of tiny, localized infections. The antibacterial wash mechanically removes the surface bacteria, and the ointment targets the deeper infected pores.
Actionable Steps: Throw away your current razor immediately, as it is heavily contaminated. Do not shave the area again until the skin is 100% healed. Wear strictly loose, breathable cotton clothing to eliminate friction and allow the skin to dry completely.

